"ZwSetSecurityObject function (ntifs.h) - Windows drivers | Microsoft Learn (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/ddi/ntifs/nf-ntifs-zwsetsecurityobject)\nciteturn47view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/ddi/ntifs/nf-ntifs-zwsetsecurityobject\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 143\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】 【1†Skip to Ask Learn chat experience】\nL1: \nL2: This browser is no longer supported.\nL3: \nL4: Upgrade to Microsoft Edge to take advantage of the latest features, security updates, and technical support.\nL5: \nL6: 【2†Download Microsoft Edge†go.microsoft.com】 【3†More info about Internet Explorer and Microsoft Edge】\nL7: \nL8: Table of contents Exit editor mode\nL9: \nL10: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL11: \nL12: Reading mode Table of contents 【1†Read in English】 Add Add to Plans 【4†Edit†github.com】\nL13: \nL14: * * *\nL15: \nL16: Copy Markdown Print\nL17: \nL18: * * *\nL19: \nL20: Note\nL21: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try 【1†signing in】 or changing directories.\nL22: \nL23: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try changing directories.\nL24: # ZwSetSecurityObject function (ntifs.h)\nL25: \nL26: Feedback\nL27: \nL28: Summarize this article for me\nL29: \nL30: ## In this article\nL31: \nL32: The ZwSetSecurityObject routine sets an object's security state.\nL33: \nL34: ## Syntax\nL35: \nL36:     `NTSYSAPI NTSTATUS ZwSetSecurityObject(\nL37:       [in] HANDLE               Handle,\nL38:       [in] SECURITY_INFORMATION SecurityInformation,\nL39:       [in] PSECURITY_DESCRIPTOR SecurityDescriptor\nL40:     );\nL41:     `\nL42: ## Parameters\nL43: \nL44: `[in] Handle`\nL45: \nL46: Handle for the object whose security state is to be set. This handle must have the access specified in the Meaning column of the table shown in the description of the SecurityInformation parameter.\nL47: \nL48: `[in] SecurityInformation`\nL49: \nL50: A 【5†SECURITY_INFORMATION】value specifying the information to be set. Can be a combination of one or more of the following values.\nL51: Value  | Meaning\nL52: --- | ---\nL53: DACL_SECURITY_INFORMATION  | Indicates the discretionary access control list (DACL) of the object is to be set. Requires WRITE_DAC access.\nL54: GROUP_SECURITY_INFORMATION  | Indicates the primary group identifier of the object is to be set. Requires WRITE_OWNER access.\nL55: OWNER_SECURITY_INFORMATION  | Indicates the owner identifier of the object is to be set. Requires WRITE_OWNER access.\nL56: SACL_SECURITY_INFORMATION  | Indicates the system ACL (SACL) of the object is to be set. Requires ACCESS_SYSTEM_SECURITY access.\nL57: `[in] SecurityDescriptor`\nL58: \nL59: Pointer to the security descriptor to be set for the object.\nL60: ## Return value\nL61: \nL62: ZwSetSecurityObject returns STATUS_SUCCESS or an appropriate error status. Possible error status codes include the following:\nL63: Return code  | Description\nL64: --- | ---\nL65: STATUS_ACCESS_DENIED  | Handle does not have the required access rights.\nL66: STATUS_ACCESS_VIOLATION  | SecurityDescriptor is a NULL pointer.\nL67: STATUS_INSUFFICIENT_RESOURCES  | The object's security descriptor could not be captured.\nL68: STATUS_INVALID_ACL  | The object's security descriptor contains an invalid ACL.\nL69: STATUS_INVALID_HANDLE  | Handle is not a valid handle.\nL70: STATUS_INVALID_SECURITY_DESCR  | SecurityDescriptor does not point to a valid security descriptor.\nL71: STATUS_INVALID_SID  | The object's security descriptor contains an invalid SID.\nL72: STATUS_OBJECT_TYPE_MISMATCH  | Handle is not a handle of the expected type.\nL73: STATUS_UNKNOWN_REVISION  | The revision level of the object's security descriptor is unknown or is not supported.\nL74: ## Remarks\nL75: \nL76: A security descriptor can be in absolute or self-relative form. In self-relative form, all members of the structure are located contiguously in memory. In absolute form, the structure only contains pointers to the members. For more information, see \"Absolute and Self-Relative Security Descriptors\" in the Security section of the Windows SDK documentation.\nL77: For more information about security and access control, see 【6†Windows security model for driver developers】 and the documentation on these topics in the Windows SDK.\nL78: \nL79: Minifilters should use 【7†FltSetSecurityObject】 instead of ZwSetSecurityObject.\nL80: \nL81: Callers of ZwSetSecurityObject must be running at IRQL = PASSIVE_LEVEL and 【8†with special kernel APCs enabled】.\nL82: \nL83: Note\nL84: If the call to the ZwSetSecurityObject function occurs in user mode, you should use the name \"【9†NtSetSecurityObject】\" instead of \"ZwSetSecurityObject\".\nL85: For calls from kernel-mode drivers, the NtXxx and ZwXxx versions of a Windows Native System Services routine can behave differently in the way that they handle and interpret input parameters. For more information about the relationship between the NtXxx and ZwXxx versions of a routine, see 【10†Using Nt and Zw Versions of the Native System Services Routines】.\nL86: ## Requirements\nL87: \nL88: Requirement  | Value\nL89: --- | ---\nL90: Minimum supported client  | Windows XP\nL91: Target Platform  | Universal\nL92: Header  | ntifs.h (include Ntifs.h)\nL93: Library  | NtosKrnl.lib\nL94: DLL  | NtosKrnl.exe\nL95: IRQL  | PASSIVE_LEVEL (see Remarks section)\nL96: DDI compliance rules  | 【11†HwStorPortProhibitedDDIs(storport)】, 【12†PowerIrpDDis(wdm)】\nL97: ## See also\nL98: \nL99: 【7†FltSetSecurityObject】\nL100: \nL101: 【13†SECURITY_DESCRIPTOR】\nL102: \nL103: 【5†SECURITY_INFORMATION】\nL104: \nL105: 【10†Using Nt and Zw Versions of the Native System Services Routines】\nL106: \nL107: 【14†ZwQuerySecurityObject】\nL108: \nL109: * * *\nL110: \nL111: ## Feedback\nL112: \nL113: Was this page helpful?\nL114: \nL115: Yes No No\nL116: \nL117: Need help with this topic?\nL118: \nL119: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL120: \nL121: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL122: \nL123: Suggest a fix?\nL124: \nL125: * * *\nL126: \nL127: ## Additional resources\nL128: \nL129: * * *\nL130: \nL131:   * Last updated on 2023-02-22\nL132: ### In this article\nL133: \nL134: Was this page helpful?\nL135: \nL136: Need help with this topic?\nL137: \nL138: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL139: \nL140: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL141: \nL142: Suggest a fix?\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSetFileSecurityW - Win32 apps | Microsoft Learn (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/securitybaseapi/nf-securitybaseapi-setfilesecurityw)\nciteturn47view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/securitybaseapi/nf-securitybaseapi-setfilesecurityw\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 123\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】 【1†Skip to Ask Learn chat experience】\nL1: \nL2: This browser is no longer supported.\nL3: \nL4: Upgrade to Microsoft Edge to take advantage of the latest features, security updates, and technical support.\nL5: \nL6: 【2†Download Microsoft Edge†go.microsoft.com】 【3†More info about Internet Explorer and Microsoft Edge】\nL7: \nL8: Table of contents Exit editor mode\nL9: \nL10: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL11: \nL12: Reading mode Table of contents 【1†Read in English】 Add Add to Plans 【4†Edit†github.com】\nL13: \nL14: * * *\nL15: \nL16: Copy Markdown Print\nL17: \nL18: * * *\nL19: \nL20: Note\nL21: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try 【1†signing in】 or changing directories.\nL22: \nL23: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try changing directories.\nL24: # SetFileSecurityW function (securitybaseapi.h)\nL25: \nL26: Feedback\nL27: \nL28: Summarize this article for me\nL29: \nL30: ## In this article\nL31: \nL32: The SetFileSecurity function sets the security of a file or directory object.\nL33: \nL34: This function is obsolete. Use the 【5†SetNamedSecurityInfo】 function instead.\nL35: \nL36: ## Syntax\nL37: \nL38:     `BOOL SetFileSecurityW(\nL39:       [in] LPCWSTR              lpFileName,\nL40:       [in] SECURITY_INFORMATION SecurityInformation,\nL41:       [in] PSECURITY_DESCRIPTOR pSecurityDescriptor\nL42:     );\nL43:     `\nL44: ## Parameters\nL45: \nL46: `[in] lpFileName`\nL47: \nL48: A pointer to a null-terminated string that specifies the file or directory for which security is set. Note that security applied to a directory is not inherited by its children.\nL49: \nL50: `[in] SecurityInformation`\nL51: \nL52: Specifies a 【6†SECURITY_INFORMATION】 structure that identifies the contents of the 【7†security descriptor】 pointed to by the pSecurityDescriptor parameter.\nL53: \nL54: `[in] pSecurityDescriptor`\nL55: \nL56: A pointer to a 【8†SECURITY_DESCRIPTOR】 structure.\nL57: ## Return value\nL58: \nL59: If the function succeeds, the function returns nonzero.\nL60: \nL61: If the function fails, it returns zero. To get extended error information, call 【9†GetLastError】.\nL62: ## Remarks\nL63: \nL64: The SetFileSecurity function is successful only if the following conditions are met:\nL65:   * If the owner of the object is being set, the calling 【10†process】 must have either WRITE_OWNER permission or be the owner of the object.\nL66:   * If the 【11†discretionary access control list】 (DACL) of the object is being set, the calling process must have either WRITE_DAC permission or be the owner of the object.\nL67:   * If the 【7†system access control list】 (SACL) of the object is being set, the SE_SECURITY_NAME privilege must be enabled for the calling process.\nL68: ## Requirements\nL69: \nL70: Requirement  | Value\nL71: --- | ---\nL72: Minimum supported client  | Windows XP [desktop apps only]\nL73: Minimum supported server  | Windows Server 2003 [desktop apps only]\nL74: Target Platform  | Windows\nL75: Header  | securitybaseapi.h (include Windows.h)\nL76: Library  | Advapi32.lib\nL77: DLL  | Advapi32.dll\nL78: ## See also\nL79: \nL80: 【12†GetFileSecurity】\nL81: 【13†Low-level Access Control】\nL82: 【14†Low-level Access Control Functions】\nL83: 【8†SECURITY_DESCRIPTOR】\nL84: 【6†SECURITY_INFORMATION】\nL85: 【15†SetKernelObjectSecurity】\nL86: 【5†SetNamedSecurityInfo】 【16†SetPrivateObjectSecurity】\nL87: 【17†SetUserObjectSecurity】\nL88: \nL89: * * *\nL90: \nL91: ## Feedback\nL92: \nL93: Was this page helpful?\nL94: \nL95: Yes No No\nL96: \nL97: Need help with this topic?\nL98: \nL99: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL100: \nL101: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL102: \nL103: Suggest a fix?\nL104: \nL105: * * *\nL106: ## Additional resources\nL107: \nL108: * * *\nL109: \nL110:   * Last updated on 2022-12-09\nL111: \nL112: ### In this article\nL113: \nL114: Was this page helpful?\nL115: \nL116: Need help with this topic?\nL117: \nL118: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL119: \nL120: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL121: \nL122: Suggest a fix?\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSECURITY_INFORMATION (Winnt.h) - Win32 apps | Microsoft Learn (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/secauthz/security-information)\nciteturn47view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/secauthz/security-information\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 188\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】 【1†Skip to Ask Learn chat experience】\nL1: \nL2: This browser is no longer supported.\nL3: \nL4: Upgrade to Microsoft Edge to take advantage of the latest features, security updates, and technical support.\nL5: \nL6: 【2†Download Microsoft Edge†go.microsoft.com】 【3†More info about Internet Explorer and Microsoft Edge】\nL7: \nL8: Table of contents Exit editor mode\nL9: \nL10: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL11: \nL12: Reading mode Table of contents 【1†Read in English】 Add Add to Plans 【4†Edit†github.com】\nL13: \nL14: * * *\nL15: \nL16: Copy Markdown Print\nL17: \nL18: * * *\nL19: \nL20: Note\nL21: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try 【1†signing in】 or changing directories.\nL22: \nL23: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try changing directories.\nL24: # SECURITY_INFORMATION\nL25: \nL26: Feedback\nL27: \nL28: Summarize this article for me\nL29: \nL30: ## In this article\nL31: \nL32: The SECURITY_INFORMATION data type identifies the object-related security information being set or queried. This security information includes:\nL33: \nL34:   * The owner of an object\nL35:   * The primary group of an object\nL36:   * The 【5†discretionary access control list】 (DACL) of an object\nL37:   * The 【6†system access control list】 (SACL) of an object\nL38: \nL39:     `typedef DWORD SECURITY_INFORMATION, *PSECURITY_INFORMATION;\nL40:     `\nL41: ## Remarks\nL42: \nL43: Some SECURITY_INFORMATION members work only with the 【7†SetNamedSecurityInfo】 function. These members are not returned in the structure returned by other security functions such as 【8†GetNamedSecurityInfo】 or 【9†ConvertStringSecurityDescriptorToSecurityDescriptor】.\nL44: \nL45: Each item of security information is designated by a bit flag. Each bit flag can be one of the following values. For more information, see the 【10†SetSecurityAccessMask】 and 【11†QuerySecurityAccessMask】 functions.\nL46: Value/rights required to query/set  | Meaning\nL47: --- | ---\nL48: ATTRIBUTE_SECURITY_INFORMATION\nL49: Right required to query: READ_CONTROL\nL50: Right required to set: WRITE_DAC\nL51:  | The resource properties of the object being referenced. The resource properties are stored in SYSTEM_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTE_ACE types in the SACL of the security descriptor.\nL52: Windows Server 2008 R2, Windows 7, Windows Server 2008, Windows Vista, Windows Server 2003 and Windows XP: This bit flag is not available.\nL53: BACKUP_SECURITY_INFORMATION\nL54: Right required to query: READ_CONTROL and ACCESS_SYSTEM_SECURITY\nL55: Right required to set: WRITE_DAC and WRITE_OWNER and ACCESS_SYSTEM_SECURITY\nL56:  | All parts of the security descriptor. This is useful for backup and restore software that needs to preserve the entire security descriptor.\nL57: Windows Server 2008 R2, Windows 7, Windows Server 2008, Windows Vista, Windows Server 2003 and Windows XP: This bit flag is not available.\nL58: DACL_SECURITY_INFORMATION\nL59: Right required to query: READ_CONTROL\nL60: Right required to set: WRITE_DAC\nL61:  | The DACL of the object is being referenced.\nL62: GROUP_SECURITY_INFORMATION\nL63: Right required to query: READ_CONTROL\nL64: Right required to set: WRITE_OWNER\nL65:  | The primary group identifier of the object is being referenced.\nL66: LABEL_SECURITY_INFORMATION\nL67: Right required to query: READ_CONTROL\nL68: Right required to set: WRITE_OWNER\nL69:  | The mandatory integrity label is being referenced.\nL70: The mandatory integrity label is an ACE in the SACL of the object.\nL71: Windows Server 2003 and Windows XP: This bit flag is not available.\nL72: OWNER_SECURITY_INFORMATION\nL73: Right required to query: READ_CONTROL\nL74: Right required to set: WRITE_OWNER\nL75:  | The owner identifier of the object is being referenced.\nL76: PROTECTED_DACL_SECURITY_INFORMATION\nL77: Right required to query: Not available\nL78: Right required to set: WRITE_DAC\nL79:  | The DACL cannot inherit 【12†access control entries】 (ACEs).\nL80: PROTECTED_SACL_SECURITY_INFORMATION\nL81: Right required to query: Not available\nL82: Right required to set: ACCESS_SYSTEM_SECURITY\nL83:  | The SACL cannot inherit ACEs.\nL84: SACL_SECURITY_INFORMATION\nL85: Right required to query: ACCESS_SYSTEM_SECURITY\nL86: Right required to set: ACCESS_SYSTEM_SECURITY\nL87:  | The SACL of the object is being referenced.\nL88: SCOPE_SECURITY_INFORMATION\nL89: Right required to query: READ_CONTROL\nL90: Right required to set: ACCESS_SYSTEM_SECURITY\nL91:  | The Central Access Policy (CAP) identifier applicable on the object that is being referenced. Each CAP identifier is stored in a SYSTEM_SCOPED_POLICY_ID_ACE type in the SACL of the SD.\nL92: Windows Server 2008 R2, Windows 7, Windows Server 2008, Windows Vista, Windows Server 2003 and Windows XP: This bit flag is not available.\nL93: UNPROTECTED_DACL_SECURITY_INFORMATION\nL94: Right required to query: Not available\nL95: Right required to set: WRITE_DAC\nL96:  | The DACL inherits ACEs from the parent object.\nL97: UNPROTECTED_SACL_SECURITY_INFORMATION\nL98: Right required to query: Not available\nL99: Right required to set: ACCESS_SYSTEM_SECURITY\nL100:  | The SACL inherits ACEs from the parent object.\nL101: ## Requirements\nL102: \nL103: Requirement  | Value\nL104: --- | ---\nL105: Minimum supported client\nL106:  | Windows XP [desktop apps only]\nL107: Minimum supported server\nL108:  | Windows Server 2003 [desktop apps only]\nL109: Header\nL110:  |\nL111: \nL112: Winnt.h (include Windows.h)\nL113: ## See also\nL114: \nL115: 【13†Access Control】\nL116: \nL117: 【14†Basic Access Control Structures】\nL118: \nL119: 【15†ConvertSecurityDescriptorToStringSecurityDescriptor】\nL120: \nL121: 【9†ConvertStringSecurityDescriptorToSecurityDescriptor】\nL122: \nL123: 【16†GetFileSecurity】\nL124: \nL125: 【17†GetKernelObjectSecurity】\nL126: \nL127: 【8†GetNamedSecurityInfo】\nL128: \nL129: 【18†GetPrivateObjectSecurity】\nL130: \nL131: 【19†GetSecurityInfo】\nL132: \nL133: 【20†GetUserObjectSecurity】\nL134: \nL135: 【11†QuerySecurityAccessMask】\nL136: \nL137: 【21†SetFileSecurity】\nL138: \nL139: 【22†SetKernelObjectSecurity】\nL140: \nL141: 【7†SetNamedSecurityInfo】\nL142: \nL143: 【23†SetPrivateObjectSecurity】\nL144: 【10†SetSecurityAccessMask】\nL145: \nL146: 【24†SetSecurityInfo】\nL147: \nL148: 【25†SetUserObjectSecurity】\nL149: \nL150: 【26†TreeResetNamedSecurityInfo】\nL151: \nL152: 【27†TreeSetNamedSecurityInfo】\nL153: \nL154: * * *\nL155: ## Feedback\nL156: \nL157: W